APP.PY : This script is a foundational Python Flask API. While simple, it illustrates a standard microservice architecture. From a DevOps perspective, it establishes an 
explicit listening configuration on port 5000 across all interfaces via 0.0.0.0, making it fully ready to receive routed traffic inside a Docker container or an AWS EC2 
instance. The JSON response it generates acts as a reliable health check endpoint for monitoring tools and cloud load balancers.
a. from flask import Flask, jsonify
Flask is the core class used to initialize the web application framework.
jsonify is a helper function that converts Python dictionaries or variables into a JSON-formatted response with the correct HTTP header.
b. app = Flask(__name__)
This creates an instance of the Flask class. The __name__ argument tells Flask where to look for resources like templates and static files. In single-module applications 
like this, it acts as a unique identifier for the app package.
@app.route('/')
def home():
    return jsonify(status="healthy", message="Flask Devops App Operational")
@app.route('/') is a Python decorator that binds the root URL path (/) to the home() function. When a user or load balancer hits the server's IP at the root level, 
this function triggers.
The home() function returns a JSON payload indicating that the application is operational.
if __name__ == '__main__':
    app.run(host='0.0.0.0', port=5000)
if __name__ == '__main__': ensures that the development server only boots up if the script is run directly (e.g., python app.py) rather than imported as a module by 
another script.
app.run(...) starts the built-in development web server.
host='0.0.0.0': This tells the Flask framework to bind to all available network interfaces on the host operating system. If it were left as default (127.0.0.1), the app 
would only accept loopback traffic from inside its own local system. Binding to 0.0.0.0 allows outside entities (such as your host computer accessing a Docker container, 
or an external AWS target group) to communicate with the app. 
port=5000: This instructs the application process to listen on TCP port 5000.


